Please first justify your definition of "unavoidable death" in view of the
above before saying anyone else is handwaving.

>How about looking at empirical data, we have loads of that?
>Has anyone actually seen an unavoidable death?

How about looking at the source code? It is plainly obvious that there is
a non-zero chance that a goblin and a wand of death are generated on DL 1,
that the goblin picks up the wand of death, that the player encounters it
in a dark area, and that the first zap hits. It is plainly obvious that
searching is not guaranteed to succeed and that some traps elegible for
generation on DL 1 can deliver fatal damage to starting characters.

>If so, give us a ttyrec and dumplog, please.

This is both unnecessary and unlikely to succeed - what we really mean is
that some situations have a chance of death even with perfect play. Any
ttyrec can be subject to the sort of prior-knowledge analysis that would
lead us, in the case above, to say "ooh, but you should have opened the
other door".

>Consider this: is it an "unavoidable" death if a monk goes to quest
>level 5 and dies to a 100 point hit from Master Kaen before he gets a
>move? 

No, but that's not something anyone is claiming is an unavoidable death.
What was that about straw men?

>going to the Castle level.  If a player should be expected to play
>well enough to anticipate a purple lich or Master Kaen on a given
